WN.chunk(30,{"n":[[1205,"Use a simple calming strategy when upset — slow 'smell the flower, blow out the candle' breaths, a cuddle, a quiet corner, squeezing a soft toy — with a grown-up's help",["Does three slow breaths when reminded","Chooses a calm-down activity they like","Calms enough to say what is wrong"],[]],[1206,"Wait a short time for something they want (a turn on the slide, a snack, a grown-up to finish speaking) using a strategy such as counting, singing or watching a timer",["Waits for a short turn without grabbing","Uses counting or a song to help them wait","Says 'my turn next' instead of pushing in"],[]],[1207,"Keep going when something is hard or goes wrong (a puzzle piece won't fit, they fall off a scooter) by having another go or asking for help instead of giving up",["Has another go after a first try fails","Says 'I can't do it yet' or asks for help","Shows pride after succeeding at something tricky"],[]],[1208,"Understand that change is a normal part of life — such as starting school, getting a new teacher, a new baby arriving, or moving house — and identify simple strategies that help them cope with changes",["Name at least two changes they have experienced and how they felt","Identify a person they can talk to when a change feels hard","Describe one strategy that helps them cope with change, such as keeping a familiar routine"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P2-7"]],[1209,"Understand that making mistakes is a normal part of learning and that everyone — including adults — makes mistakes, and begin to see mistakes as opportunities to learn rather than reasons to give up",["Describe a time they made a mistake and what they learned from it","Respond to a mistake by trying again rather than giving up","Explain why mistakes help people learn"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-CU-P1-2","ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P1-9"]],[1210,"Use simple calming strategies when feeling upset or overwhelmed — such as taking deep breaths, counting to ten, or going to a quiet space — and understand that these help the body and mind settle down",["Demonstrate at least two calming strategies when prompted","Choose to use a calming strategy independently during a real upset","Explain in simple terms why calming down helps them think better"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P1-7"]],[1211,"Know and use the key words for managing big feelings — calm, strategy, cope, settle, patience, overwhelmed, and breathe — and understand that having words for these ideas is the first step to using them",["Use words like 'frustrated', 'anxious', 'overwhelmed', and 'disappointed' accurately to describe how they feel in difficult moments","Explain what 'calm down' strategies are in their own words and name at least two they have tried (e.g. deep breaths, counting, walking away)","Distinguish between similar feelings — e.g. explain the difference between being angry and being disappointed"],[]],[1212,"Break a challenging task into smaller, manageable steps rather than feeling overwhelmed by the whole thing — and celebrate progress along the way",["Take a challenging task and list at least three smaller steps to complete it","Start with the first step rather than procrastinating on the whole task","Acknowledge progress after completing each step"],[]],[1213,"Understand that different situations require different coping strategies — what works for anger might not work for sadness, and what helps at school might be different from what helps at home",["Name at least two different coping strategies and explain when each one is most useful","Choose an appropriate strategy based on the specific situation and emotion","Reflect on a time a strategy didn't work and explain what they might try instead"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-CU-P2-7"]],[1214,"Understand the concept of a growth mindset — that abilities and intelligence can grow with effort, practice, and good strategies — as opposed to a fixed mindset where you believe you're either good at something or you're not",["Explain the difference between growth mindset and fixed mindset in their own words","Use 'yet' language when describing something they find difficult","Give an example of something they got better at through practice and effort"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-CU-P2-4","ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P2-12"]],[1215,"Use positive self-talk to manage difficult situations — replacing unhelpful thoughts like 'I'm stupid' or 'I'll never be able to do this' with encouraging ones like 'This is hard but I can keep trying' or 'I've done hard things before'",["Give an example of an unhelpful thought and rephrase it as a helpful one","Use positive self-talk aloud or in writing when facing a challenge","Explain how the words we say to ourselves affect how we feel and perform"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P2-11","ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P3-10"]],[1216,"Know and use the vocabulary of resilience and self-management — including regulate, resilience, growth mindset, fixed mindset, self-talk, trigger, setback, persevere, and distress — and understand what each word means in practice",["Explain what 'resilience' means in their own words and give an example of a time they kept going when something was hard","Use 'growth mindset' correctly — e.g. 'I can't do it yet, but I can improve with practice'","Define 'self-regulation' and describe a strategy they use to manage frustration or setbacks"],[]],[1217,"Reflect on which self-regulation and coping strategies work best for them personally, building a 'toolkit' of approaches they can draw on in different situations and sharing what works with others",["Describe at least three personal coping strategies and when each is most effective","Recommend a strategy to a friend based on the friend's specific situation","Evaluate whether a strategy they tried worked well and explain their reasoning"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-CU-P3-6","ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-CU-P4-7"]],[1218,"Set realistic personal goals, create a simple plan to achieve them, monitor their own progress, and adjust their approach when things aren't working",["Write or state a specific, achievable personal goal with a timeframe","Describe the steps they will take to reach the goal","Review their progress and adjust their plan when something isn't working"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P2-5"]],[1219,"Understand resilience as the ability to recover from setbacks, adapt to difficult circumstances, and keep going — recognising that resilience is a skill that develops through experience, not a trait you either have or don't",["Define resilience in their own words and explain why it matters","Describe a time they bounced back from a setback and what helped them recover","Explain that resilience grows through experience and that struggling doesn't mean you're weak"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-CU-P4-8","ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P4-7"]],[1220,"Manage their own time and attention effectively — prioritising tasks, minimising distractions, and maintaining focus on important work even when it's not the most exciting option",["Create a simple plan for completing multiple tasks in a sensible order","Identify their own common distractions and describe strategies to manage them","Complete a less enjoyable task before a more enjoyable one without constant reminders"],["ib-pyp-pspe:PSPE-ID-LO-P3-12"]],[1221,"Distinguish between eustress (the productive, motivating kind of stress) and distress (harmful, overwhelming stress); explain the physiological stress response (fight-flight-freeze, HPA axis) and how chronic stress affects the body and mind; identify common adolescent stressors (academic pressure, social comparison, physical change, uncertainty about the future); evaluate evidence-based coping strategies (exercise, sleep, mindfulness, social support, expressive writing); recognise warning signs that stress has crossed into anxiety or depression and know where to get help",[],[]],[1222,"Understand habit formation through the cue-routine-reward loop and how to design new habits intentionally; distinguish intrinsic motivation (doing something for its own value) from extrinsic motivation (rewards/punishments) and understand when each is more effective; understand procrastination as primarily an emotion regulation problem (avoiding discomfort) rather than a time management failure; apply self-determination theory (autonomy, competence, relatedness) to boost intrinsic motivation; design environments that reduce friction for desired behaviours",[],[]],[1223,"Understand that facing serious challenges can lead to genuine growth in three domains: new perspectives on life, improved relationships, and a strengthened sense of personal capability (post-traumatic growth); distinguish genuine growth from toxic positivity ('everything happens for a reason') and from denial; understand that resilience does not mean being unaffected by adversity but recovering and growing through it; develop a personal philosophy for handling setbacks based on meaning-making; explore how to support others going through serious difficulty without minimising their experience",[],[]],[1224,"Recognise signs of stress during exam periods and use helpful coping strategies (planning, sleep, exercise, talking, breathing) instead of avoidance",["Names physical and emotional signs of stress","Uses two coping strategies that work for them","Knows when stress is too much and who to talk to"],[]],[1225,"Understand why teenagers need 8-10 hours of sleep, how late screens and caffeine affect it, and build habits that protect sleep and mood",["Explains the teenage body-clock shift","Describes how screens affect sleep","Sets a realistic wind-down routine"],[]],[1226,"Prepare for and cope with major transitions (leaving school, moving away, starting work or university): anticipate challenges, build support networks and look after themselves",["Names challenges of a coming transition","Plans how to build a support network","Describes how they handled a past change"],[]]],"r":[[14,"Advanced resilience skills depends on foundational advanced self-regulation"],[15,"Advanced resilience skills depends on earlier coping strategies"],[24,"Advanced self-regulation depends on earlier self-control skills"],[25,"Advanced self-regulation depends on earlier foundational self-regulation"],[108,"Positive self-talk practice requires knowing the term 'self-talk' and distinguishing it from intrusive negative thoughts"],[109,"Positive self-talk builds on basic calming strategies"],[110,"Self-talk benefits from wider emotion vocabulary to name what you're feeling"],[111,"Self-talk benefits from growth mindset framing"],[319,"Resilience concept builds on coping with change"],[320,"Resilience as a concept requires understanding 'resilience', 'setback', 'persevere', and 'regulate' as precise terms"],[321,"Resilience builds on having multiple coping strategies"],[322,"Resilience benefits from understanding emotions influence decisions"],[339,"Coping with change draws on vocabulary like 'cope', 'strategy', and 'settle'"],[451,"Goal-setting and progress monitoring uses vocabulary of self-regulation and managing distress when things go wrong"],[452,"PSD goal-setting scaffolds the learning goal practices taught in Learning-to-Learn"],[453,"Goal-setting builds on ability to break tasks into steps"],[758,"Time management builds on breaking tasks into steps"],[826,"Calming strategies benefit from naming the emotion you're trying to manage"],[827,"Calming strategies (calm, breathe, settle) rely on knowing this vocabulary to name and apply the techniques"],[840,"Self-regulation mastery depends on advanced resilience skills"],[841,"Advanced self-regulation mastery depends on earlier self-regulation skills"],[854,"Distinguishing different coping strategies requires the vocabulary of regulation, triggers, and distress"],[855,"Different coping strategies builds on knowing basic calming strategies"],[856,"Choosing coping strategies benefits from reading body-emotion signals"],[858,"Coping toolkit reflection builds on understanding resilience"],[859,"Reflecting on strategies benefits from goal-setting experience"],[860,"Building a personal toolkit of self-regulation strategies requires first having noticed the patterns of situations that trigger you and the reactions you typically have"],[861,"Building a personal toolkit of self-regulation strategies and evaluating which work best is the PSD form of the universal strategy-evaluation habit"],[1021,"Breaking tasks into steps is a self-regulation strategy; vocabulary of being overwhelmed and managing setbacks is helpful"],[1022,"The SEL skill of breaking a big task into manageable steps parallels and builds on the mathematical practice of planning a step-by-step approach to complex problems"],[1023,"Breaking tasks into steps builds on growth mindset perseverance"],[1186,"The growth mindset concept requires understanding the vocabulary pair 'growth mindset' vs 'fixed mindset'"],[1187,"Growth mindset builds on understanding mistakes as learning opportunities"],[1188,"Growth mindset understanding (SEL) is grounded in the concrete experience of persevering through mathematical problems — the abstract principle is made real through mathematics"],[1483,"Framing mistakes as learning uses the vocabulary of feelings management and coping with setback"],[3904,"Noticing the feeling in their body tells them it is time to calm down"],[3905,"Waiting is easier when they can calm the strong feeling of wanting"],[3906,"Calming the frustrated feeling comes before having another go"],[4028,"Exam stress applies understanding of good and bad stress"],[4029,"It uses their personal coping toolkit"],[4030,"Changing sleep routines draws on habits and motivation"],[4054,"Transitions draw on growth through adversity"],[4055,"Looking after mental health helps through change"],[4077,"Simple calming strategies start with big breaths and a grown-up's help"],[4078,"Learning from mistakes builds on having another go"],[4095,"Learning from mistakes needs a willingness to have a go"]],"c":{"Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|5":"Your child is learning important life skills for managing emotions and challenges — using calming strategies when upset, practising patience, adapting to changes, and understanding that mistakes are a normal part of learning.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|7":"Your child is learning that they can get better at things through practice and effort, while developing strategies to stay positive and break big challenges into smaller, manageable steps.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|9":"Your child is developing important life skills — learning to manage their emotions and behavior, set and work toward goals, and build resilience to bounce back from challenges and setbacks.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|3":"Your child is learning to calm down with slow breaths or a cuddle and to wait a short time for a turn.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|4":"Your child is learning to have another go when something goes wrong, instead of giving up.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|14":"Your child is learning to manage exam stress and protect sleep and wellbeing.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Regulation & Resilience|16":"Your child is preparing to cope with big transitions such as leaving school, starting work or moving away."}});
